Let $A=\{a_1,\ldots,a_s\}\subset\mathbb R^d$ be a finite set of at least two points with affine span $\mathbb R^d$, and define $p_i,F,B,m_F$ as above. Then $A$ is Ramsey if and only if there is a matrix $P\in\operatorname{Mat}_{d+1}(B)$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 (p_i\otimes1)^T P(1\otimes p_i)&=0
       &&(1\le i\le s),\label{eq:field-evaluations}\\
 m_F(P_{\alpha\beta})&=\delta_{\alpha\beta}
       &&(1\le\alpha,\beta\le d).\label{eq:field-spatial}
\end{aligned}$$ Here the entries of $p_i\otimes1$ and $1\otimes p_i$ are formed componentwise. There are no separate conditions on the multiplied constant row or constant column of $P$.

Let $k,s\geq1$, let $\Lambda_1,\ldots,\Lambda_s$ be subgroups of $\Gamma=\mathbb Z^k$, and put $V_i=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb Q}\Lambda_i$. Let $C\in\operatorname{Mat}_k(\mathbb Q)$ be symmetric. Suppose that the image of $\sum_{a,b}C_{ab}\varepsilon_a\otimes\varepsilon_b$ in $(\mathbb Q^k/V_i)\otimes_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb Q^k/V_i)$ is zero for every $i$, where $\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_k$ is the standard basis of $\mathbb Q^k$. There is a finitely supported function $\nu:\Gamma\to\mathbb Q$ such that $$\label{stencil:cosets}
 \sum_{\gamma\in q}\nu(\gamma)=0
 \qquad(q\in\Gamma/\Lambda_i,\ 1\leq i\leq s)$$ and $$\label{stencil:moments}
 \sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)=0,\qquad
 \sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)\gamma=0,\qquad
 \sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)\gamma\gamma^{\mathsf T}=C.$$

Let $A=\{a_1,\ldots,a_s\}\subset\mathbb R^d$ have affine span $\mathbb R^d$, with $s\geq2$. Let $W=\mathbb R^{1+d}$ be regarded as a vector space over $\mathbb Q$. Suppose that a symmetric tensor $T\in W\otimes_{\mathbb Q}W$ satisfies $$(e_i\otimes e_i)(T)=0\quad(1\leq i\leq s),
 \qquad G(T)=I_d,$$ where $e_i(v,u)=v+a_i\cdot u$. For every $\eta>0$, there are an integer $\ell\geq1$ and vectors $f_i,g_i\in\mathbb R^\ell$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \|f_i-f_j\|^2&=\|a_i-a_j\|^2,
 &\|g_i-g_j\|^2&=q\|a_i-a_j\|^2,
 &q&=\frac{\eta}{1+\eta}, \label{pairs:distances}
\end{aligned}$$ for every $i,j$, and $g_i$ is a permutation of the coordinates of $f_i$ for each $i$.

Let $\Gamma=\mathbb Z^k$, and let $\Lambda_1,\ldots,\Lambda_s$ be subgroups of $\Gamma$. Suppose that a finitely supported function $\nu:\Gamma\to\mathbb Q$ has zero pushforward on each $\Gamma/\Lambda_i$, and satisfies $$\sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)=0,
 \qquad \sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)\gamma=0,
 \qquad \sum_\gamma\nu(\gamma)\gamma\gamma^{\mathsf T}=C.$$ Let $U$ be a real $d\times k$ matrix with $UCU^{\mathsf T}=I_d$, where $d\geq1$. For every $\eta>0$, there are an integer $n\geq1$, a finitely supported $\mu:\Gamma\to\mathbb Q$, and $\zeta\in\mathbb Q_{>0}$ such that $\mu$ has zero pushforward on each $\Gamma/\Lambda_i$ and $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_\lambda\mu(\lambda)
       \frac{\lambda}{n}\frac{\lambda^{\mathsf T}}{n}
   &=\zeta C, \label{pairs:exact-moment}\\
 \sum_\lambda\mu(\lambda)_-
       \left\|U\frac{\lambda}{n}\right\|^2
   &<\eta\zeta. \label{pairs:small-negative}
\end{aligned}$$

Let $H$ be a finite group of coordinate permutations of $\mathcal E$, and let $Z\subset\mathcal E$ be a finite $H$-invariant set. For every $\delta>0$, there is a tuple $w\in K^Z$ represented by diagonal factors and factors $(\xi_{h x})_{x\in Z}$, $h\in H$, with degree $m>0$, for which $$\label{groups:orbit-bound}
 w_xw_y^{-1}\in L,\qquad c(w_xw_y^{-1})\le\delta m
 \quad\text{whenever }x,y\text{ belong to the same }H\text{-orbit}.$$
